## 2. What depth and wow mean here

A judge runs three tests in ten minutes: is this real, does it go deep, and would a university use it. The research note on judges ([judge-experience-and-wow-2026-10-22.md](research/judge-experience-and-wow-2026-10-22.md), Section 2) says a live, working, specific demo beats a more ambitious one on slides, that a named weakness raises scores, and that the sentence a judge repeats an hour later is chosen in advance. The sentence for this track: **"They showed me the course before and after, and the receipt for why."**

**Depth** means the records a quality office keeps exist in the app and look like the real thing: the course file as EAC lists it (course information, the CLO to PLO matrix, the syllabus, references, assessments with marking schemes, rubrics, graded samples), the OBE course report with its KPI, the two surveys, the CQI note ([course-records-and-surveys-2026-10-22.md](research/course-records-and-surveys-2026-10-22.md), Sections 1 to 3).

**Wow** means a stored record shown so well that the judge feels the change, and never a trick. The organising pattern is Duarte's "what is, what could be": hold the old state, reveal the new with one comparison pattern, and leave the comparison on screen so the information lives in the persisted view and not in the motion ([before-and-after-patterns-2026-10-22.md](research/before-and-after-patterns-2026-10-22.md), pattern 10). The ranked list of fifteen wow moments in the judge note is folded into Section 9 with a cost each; the ones chosen for Track C are the ones that are honest by construction and cheap enough for four days.

