One string, in `src/data/sample-course/index.ts`, used wherever the mark or the course appears: "A demo course at UTP, written for this prototype. CS310 is not a UTP course outline, and these marks, responses and slides are synthetic." The real UTP course in this area, TFB3133 Data Visualisation in the 2026 handbook, is named in the research note and nowhere in the app.

## 4. The course record, to accreditation depth

### 4.1 What a complete record holds

From the research (EAC 2020 and 2024 course files; MQA GGP AOSL Table 1 and Appendix 12; UTM's Course Information elements; AUN-QA 4.0 course specification): a synopsis of 100 to 150 words; four to six CLOs, one measurable verb each, tagged with a Bloom level and an MQF cluster; the CLO to PLO matrix with a level per cell; the teaching and learning methods; the assessment plan (components, weights, the CLOs each assesses, the week) with a table of specifications; student learning time that adds to 40 hours per credit; a week-by-week schedule; references under five years old; the course materials (lecture notes, lab sheets, the project brief, rubrics, exam papers with marking schemes, graded samples at three levels); and the per-term OBE report with its CQI note. Each one maps onto a Lyceum record below; what is left out is listed in 4.5.

### 4.2 The data model (additions, all optional so old backups migrate)

| Record | Added fields | Notes |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `CourseDefinition` | `synopsis`, `methods: string[]`, `references: Reference[]`, `assessment: AssessmentComponent[]`, `slt: { lecture, lab, tutorial, selfStudy, assessment, exam }` | `slt` totals are checked against `credits * 40`; `assessment` weights are checked to sum to 100 |
| `CourseOutcome` | `mqfCluster`, `ploLinks: { ploId, level: "H" or "M" or "L" }[]` | `ploIds` stays for compatibility and is derived from `ploLinks` |
| `CourseTopic` | `kind: "lecture" or "lab" or "project" or "quiz" or "exam"`, `activities`, `readings`, `misconceptions: string[]` | `misconceptions` feed the distractors in Section 10 |
| `Programme` | `plos: { id, text, cluster }[]` | Eight demo PLOs shaped on the MQF 2.0 clusters, labelled synthetic |
| `AssessmentComponent` (new) | `id, title, kind, weight, cloIds, week, bloomLevels, description, rubricId?` | The plan's rows |
| `Rubric` (new) | `id, title, criteria: { name, levels: [capstone, milestone2, milestone1, benchmark] }[]` | The four-level grid shape of the VALUE rubrics with our own descriptors; the AAC&U text is linked, not bundled (its licence is CC BY-NC-SA) |

### 4.3 The sample course: CS310 Data Visualisation, in our words

Authored during D1 under `src/data/sample-course/cs310.ts`, written by the session in its own words, modelled on the published outlines the note lists (GWU DATS 6401 Spring 2026 for the week structure and the project; UTP's TFB3133 synopsis for the local anchor on JavaScript charts, D3 and a mini project; the BTAA rubric for the criteria), credited in a `source` line on the record, with no sentence copied from any of them. The skeleton, so every session writes the same course:

**Outcomes** (the existing `CLO5` keeps its id, because the tour, the smokes, the sample results file and the Map step target it; the four new outcomes take course-scoped ids `CS310-1` to `CS310-4` with display labels CLO1 to CLO4, and `CLO5` keeps the label CLO5):

| Label | Bloom | MQF cluster | The outcome, our wording | PLO links |
|:--|:--|:--|:--|:--|
| CLO1 | Understand | Knowledge and understanding | Explain how data and task abstraction, marks and channels, and human perception decide whether a chart answers its question | PLO1 H, PLO2 M |
| CLO2 | Apply | Functional work skills (digital) | Construct charts for categorical, temporal and geospatial data with a reproducible tool chain, with the colour and accessibility choices justified | PLO4 H, PLO6 M |
| CLO3 | Analyse | Cognitive skills | Analyse a published visualisation against perception, Gestalt, accessibility and ethics criteria and diagnose where it misleads | PLO2 H, PLO7 M |
| CLO4 | Evaluate | Cognitive skills | Evaluate competing designs for a stakeholder question using evidence from a usability check, and justify a recommendation | PLO2 H, PLO5 M |
| CLO5 | Apply (before), Create (after the tour's revision) | Functional work skills (interpersonal and communication) | Before: design and present a data story for a stakeholder audience using static visualisations. After the tour's Update: design and present an interactive dashboard and data story for a stakeholder audience, with the design choices justified | PLO5 H, PLO4 M |

The before and after of CLO5 is the demo's own change: the dashboards evidence in Report maps to CLO5 today, the decision adopts, and Update rewords the outcome. D1 decides how the tour's existing Update steps (which type into the outcome and add a topic) map onto the richer record without changing a caption, and logs it.

**Student learning time** for 3 credits, 120 hours: lecture 14 weeks by 2 hours, 28; lab 14 by 2, 28; self-study 40; assessment work 21 (quizzes 3, labs 6, project 12); final examination 3. Contact 56, so `brief.contactHours` is 56 and the Meter on the Course screen reads 56 of 56.

**Schedule**, fourteen weeks, each a `CourseTopic` with its kind, hours, outcomes, readings and two or three misconceptions:

1. Why visualise: questions, data and tasks (lecture, lab 0: the tool chain installed)
2. Data and task abstraction; tidy data
3. Marks, channels and perception
4. Colour, Gestalt and accessibility
5. Charts for comparison and distribution
6. Time: trends, seasonality and small multiples
7. Mid-semester quiz; critique workshop on misleading charts
8. Interaction: filter, brush and link; the project brief released
9. Dashboards I: layout, hierarchy, at a glance
10. Dashboards II: interactivity and publishing (the topic the tour's revision expands, within the 56 hours, by moving two lab hours from week 6)
11. Geospatial and network data
12. Uncertainty and ethics
13. Data storytelling for stakeholders
14. Project presentations and review

**Assessment plan** (continuous 60, final 40, inside the 50 to 70 continuous range UTM's guide gives): quizzes 10 (CLO1, CLO3; weeks 7 and 12), labs 15 (CLO2; weeks 2 to 11), the dashboard project 25 (CLO5 and CLO4; weeks 8 to 14, with the rubric), the critique essay 10 (CLO3; week 9), the final examination 40 (CLO1, CLO2, CLO4). A table of specifications: marks per CLO per Bloom level, computed from the components and checked to equal the weights.

**Rubric** for the project: four criteria shaped on the BTAA categories in our words (design and accessibility; clarity and integrity of the communication, sources cited; insight for the audience; reproducibility of the pipeline), four levels each.

**References**: Munzner 2014, Few 2013, and three items under five years old chosen by the session from the course's field, each with a real title, author and year the session can verify from the research notes or a known publisher page (nothing invented; if the session cannot verify a reference it leaves a two-item list and says so).

**Methods**: lectures, labs, a studio critique, the project with a defence.

### 4.4 The Course screen

The Course step grows from two panels to a record with sections, in this order, each a panel with the chunky outline and lip the owner approved: Synopsis (with the credits, the SLT bar lecture, lab, self-study, assessment, exam, totalling 120); Outcomes (five cards with the Bloom tag, the MQF cluster and the assessment that evidences each, and the CLO to PLO matrix as a heat table in H, M and L with the level in text in every cell); Assessment plan (the components with weights, the table of specifications behind a disclosure); Schedule (fourteen rows: week, kind, topic, hours, outcomes, the pack's thumbnail, a dot when a lab sheet or the brief exists); Materials (Section 5); References; the context and the preparation checklist as today behind disclosures. One action at the top stays: Watch employers, or Start a revision.

**The course file in one click.** "Export the course file (.zip)": `course-information.md` (everything above), the fourteen packs, the lab sheets, the project brief, the rubric (`.xlsx`), the quiz (`.xml`, Moodle), the OBE course report (`.md`), the decisions and versions (`.md`), and `MANIFEST.md`, a checklist in the EAC course-file order marking each item present, sample or not included. The zip is built with the `jszip` dependency already installed.

### 4.5 Left out, and said so

Graded student samples at low, medium and high (synthetic student work is one step too far from honest); exam papers beyond the week 7 quiz and the final's outline with its marking scheme; the external examiner's report. `MANIFEST.md` lists them as not included, so the course file shows its own gaps.

## 5. A semester of teaching material

### 5.1 Sessions with slides

`TeachingSession` gains `slides: SlideDraft[]` (title, bullets, notes, an optional figure: a chart from the evidence, a table, or a labelled placeholder) and `labSheet?: string` (markdown). The sample course saves fourteen sessions, one per week, with six to nine slides each written during D2 in the lecturer's voice, every slide titled, every slide with notes; the lab sheets for weeks 2 to 11; the project brief at week 8 (teams of three, a packaged workbook, a written report, a 10 to 15 minute defence, shaped on the MSU Texas brief in our words); the week 7 quiz (Section 10). The sample content is data under `src/data/sample-course/sessions.ts`, labelled "Sample slides, written for the demo".

### 5.2 The pack, with masters and the mark

`src/lib/slides.ts` keeps `DeckSpec` and gains a `Brand` record (the institution's name and mark PNG, the token colours, Calibri) and five masters through `defineSlideMaster`: TITLE (forest, the course and week, the lecturer, the mark on a white plate), SECTION, CONTENT (title, body placeholder, the mark top right, the footer with course code, week and slide number), TWO_COLUMN and CLOSING (the outcomes revisited, next week, the honesty line). `sessionDeck` builds from the saved slides first, then the evidence slide as today (the counts over the reviewed denominator and the exact quotation), then the check for understanding (the lint-passed items from Section 10), then resources. Objects are added in reading order, body text is 18 pt or larger, every slide has a unique title, every slide has notes, and shapes use the `pres.shapes` constants; the proof unzips the file and checks each of these in the XML, and only if it finds the malformed parts issue 1449 describes does D2 add the repair pass (verify, do not assume).

### 5.3 The preview on screen

`src/ui/primitives/DeckPreview.tsx` draws a `DeckSpec` to SVG at a 960 by 540 viewBox with the same layout rules as `renderDeck`: the thumbnail on each session card, a filmstrip behind "Preview the pack", the two-version comparison in Section 6, and the frames in the judge's walk. The preview says "A preview; PowerPoint draws the file" once, because it is not pixel-identical. No PowerPoint or LibreOffice is needed on the demo laptop to show slides.

### 5.4 Teach, as the judge sees it

Fourteen cards by week with the thumbnail, the outcomes, the status and the pack; a filter for the week in view; "Teaching pack (.pptx)" unchanged; the lab sheet and the brief as downloads where they exist. The tour's week 6 session step keeps its caption and now lands on a card that already has slides.

## 6. Before and after

### 6.1 One library

`src/lib/diff.ts`, deterministic and proved: `wordDiff(a, b)` (a token LCS, giving kept, deleted and inserted runs); `topicDeltas(before, after)` (per topic: hours before and after, delta, moved, added, removed); `deckDiff(a: DeckSpec, b: DeckSpec)` (per slide: title changed, bullets added or removed, figure changed, slide added or removed); `resultDeltas(prior, current)` (per outcome: share before and after); `changeSummary(...)` ("3 outcomes reworded, 2 h moved, 2 slides revised", from the diffs). The library reads records only; it never guesses.

### 6.2 The views, from the research's ten patterns

| Where | Pattern | What the judge sees | Accessibility |
|:--|:--|:--|:--|
| Update, the check panel, before adopting | Word-level redline (1) | CLO5's old words struck through in the danger token and the new words inserted in the emerald text token, inside one paragraph, with "Hide changes" for the clean text | Strikethrough and underline, not colour alone; visually hidden "deleted" and "inserted" labels |
| Update and History | Current versus proposed, two columns (2) | Topics and hours as a real table: topic, before, after, delta as text ("+2 h"), the changed cells outlined | A table with headers; the delta in words |
| Update and History | A dumbbell per topic (3) | An SVG with two dots and a bar per topic, sorted by the size of the change; value labels on both dots; the data table beneath | Labels in text; reduced motion shows the end state |
| Teach and History | Structural slide diff (6) and swipe or onion skin (5) | For the week 10 pack: the list of what changed (title, bullet, figure), each highlighting its region on the thumbnail; beside it the two SVG previews under a divider the judge drags or a fade | Arrow keys, click to position, "Show before" and "Show after" buttons (WCAG 2.5.7); the list is the accessible description |
| Learn | A slopegraph for results (4) | One line per outcome from the prior term's synthetic file to this term's, CLO5 emphasised, the rest grey at 3 to 1 | Every line end labelled in text |
| History | A version scrubber with named stops (7) | A slider across the course's versions and decisions, the stop named, who and when on hover, the diff always against the previous stop; a change links to the decision record that authorised it (through the `decision-recorded` and `version-adopted` events) | `role="slider"`, keyboard steps, the stop name announced; no autoplay |
| History and the recap | Change counters (9) | "3 outcomes reworded, 2 h moved, 2 slides revised" counting up once | Final numbers at once under reduced motion, in a live region |
| Everywhere | What is, what could be (10) | The before holds for a beat, the after reveals with one of the patterns above, and the comparison stays on screen | The persisted view carries the information |

Object-constant animated transitions (pattern 8) are kept for Track B: the dumbbell's end state and the counters are enough for four days.

### 6.3 Where the change comes from in the demo

The tour's Update adopts a version: CLO5 reworded, two lab hours moved from week 6 to week 10, week 10 linked to CLO5 and its pack revised (two slides). History then holds two versions, one decision and the events between them, so every view above has real records to show, and the judge can open the decision from the diff.

## 7. The two student surveys

### 7.1 The instruments

Two `SurveyDefinition` records in the workspace, built from the course record and the institution profile:

- **The Course Outcome Survey**: one item per CLO ("I can ..." in the outcome's words), 1 to 5, the target "4 or higher", one open comment. Run at the end of the semester.
- **The Student Self Reflection Tools Survey (SSRT)**: the teaching and learning instrument. UTP names it in a job advertisement and nowhere public, so the demo instrument is adapted from SEEQ's dimensions (learning, organisation, breadth, assessment, workload) in our words, twelve items on 1 to 5 plus two open comments, labelled "adapted; UTP's own instrument is not public".

Every question carries a stable id embedded in its text ("Q3.") so an import matches on the id, not the wording.

### 7.2 Collection without a server

Three ways, in the order the research recommends, each labelled on the Learn screen:

1. **The booth way (built for the demo)**: the local API (`server/`) gains `POST /api/survey/:token` and `GET /api/survey/:token/responses`, in memory, no identifiers; the student page is a route of the built app (`#/survey/<form>`) that reads the form from the URL fragment (lz-string, MIT; the proof checks the link stays under 2,000 characters) and posts to the laptop's address on the booth's own hotspot; the QR on screen encodes that address. Judges answer on their phones; the lecturer presses "Collect" and the responses land as a `DataSource` of kind `clo-survey` or `ssrt` with `sample: false` and "collected at the booth". Below five responses the screen says "3 of 5 responses needed; no aggregate is shown", which is itself the honest moment.
2. **The paste way (universal)**: the student page can also produce a short code the student pastes into the LMS or an email; the lecturer pastes codes into the app.
3. **The campus way (exports and imports)**: "Export for Microsoft Forms" writes a DOCX shaped for Quick Import (one heading per section, one numbered question per line, options as a list) with `jszip`; importers read Microsoft Forms XLSX (ID, Start time, Completion time, Email, Name, then one column per question), Google Forms CSV (Timestamp first, question text as headers) and Moodle Feedback CSV through the SheetJS dependency already installed, matched on the question id. The Google Forms API and a Cloudflare Worker collector are planned and say so.

### 7.3 Rules on screen

The five-response floor; free-text comments locked until the coordinator records "grades released" for the term (an event, `grades-released`, in the log, so History shows it); a consent line and a link to the institution's privacy notice on the student page (the profile holds the URL; UTP's general privacy notice is the demo link); nothing stored that identifies a student; the response rate beside n of N with Nulty's standard as the comparison ("31 of 38, 82%; Nulty asks about 58% for a class under 20 and 35% over 50; online surveys at Monash drew 31%").

### 7.4 The sample responses

Synthetic, under `src/data/sample-course/surveys.ts`: the prior term's Course Outcome Survey (31 of 38) and SSRT (29 of 38), with a dozen short comments written to sound like students and labelled synthetic; `scripts/gen-demo-files.mjs` also writes them as a Microsoft Forms style XLSX and a Google Forms style CSV so the importers have real fixtures.

## 8. Attainment, the way quality offices report it

### 8.1 The arithmetic

`src/lib/attainment.ts`, proved against a hand-computed fixture:

- **Direct, per CLO**: n, mean, spread, the count at or above the institution's pass mark, the share, its Wilson 95% interval (`wilsonInterval` in `src/lib/evidence.ts`), and KPI met or not against the student share. The default reads "CLO2: 29 of 38 students at 55 or above, 76% (61% to 87%); KPI 50% met".
- **Indirect, per CLO**: the Course Outcome Survey's mean and the share at 4 or higher, shown beside the direct figure with the gap in points and never averaged with it (plan Section 9.4).
- **The CLO to PLO roll-up**: this course's contribution to each PLO as the weighted mean of its CLO shares with H, M and L as 3, 2 and 1, labelled "this course's direct contribution; the programme figure needs every course".
- **The UAEU variant** (attainment as the share of students at 70% of each tool's maximum, weighted by the tool's weight) as a selectable method in the profile, so the method is visibly a setting.

### 8.2 What the intake keeps

The results file already holds a mark per student per outcome. At intake the interpreter computes, per outcome, n, mean, sd, the count at or above the profile's pass mark and a ten-bin histogram, and stores those; it never stores a student identifier. `ResultUpload.rows` gains optional `atOrAbove` and `bins`; a row from an older backup has neither, and the screen says "distribution not filed; file the results again to compute the KPI" rather than guessing.

### 8.3 The OBE course report

One panel on Learn, printable, and one section of the term recap: the header (course, term, version taught, the mark); the table CLO, n of N, mean, share at or above the pass mark with its interval, KPI, met; the indirect column beside it with the gap; the PLO contribution row; the response rate and the floor; the CQI note the lecturer writes (a saved record, `CqiNote`, with the term and the version, shown in History); and the sentence that stays: "No learning gain is claimed; the benchmark has not been run." The `PLANNED` list on Learn shrinks to pooling, frozen predictions and comment themes.

### 8.4 The sample results

`scripts/gen-demo-files.mjs` writes `results.xlsx` with five columns (CLO1 to CLO5) for 38 synthetic students and a second file, `results-prior.xlsx`, for the prior term, so the slopegraph has two points; the seed files the prior term's upload as a labelled, never-counted example, as the CS220 example is today.

## 9. The judge's way through, with the host

### 9.1 The host at the door

The research on mascots before professional audiences (GitHub, Mailchimp, Atlassian, Duolingo, Headspace) gives the rule, and Section 0.2 keeps it: the host greets, marks completions, names what is not built and signs off; it never explains an error and never sells. Tualang appears for the judge in five places:

1. **The judge's page**, `#/judge`, reachable from the landing page ("For judges: walk with Tualang") and from the Guide page. Tualang, greeting, size 160, one line: "Six steps, one course, one term. Walk with me, or watch." Three buttons: Walk the loop, Before and after, Ask the door. Under them, the card "What is not built", Tualang holding it, listing `REMAINING_WORK` from `src/app/sectionGuides.ts` word for word.
2. **The guided walk**: ten stops over the real app on the restored sample workspace, built on the existing demo runner (`src/demo/runner.ts`) in a new "guided" mode that waits for Next instead of a timer: Course (the record and the mark), Watch (a posting with its link and date), Report Quote (the receipt), Report Count (the interval), Decide (the record), Update (the redline and the dumbbell), Teach (the pack preview), Learn (the OBE table beside the survey), History (the scrubber), Home (the ring). At each stop a spotlight on the element, Tualang in a corner card with one line of at most twenty words and the honesty label where one applies ("synthetic marks"), Next, Back and Escape, all by keyboard. No new dependency: the runner already finds targets by `data-demo-id` and moves a cursor; driver.js stays an option in the decisions log.
3. **The phone**: the walk and the judge page work at 320 px (the smokes already check that width), so the poster's QR leads a judge's phone to the landing page and from there to Tualang.
4. **The term recap**, `#/course/:id/recap`: one printable A4 per course and term from records (plan Section 8): what employers asked for (the top skills with their denominators), what was decided (the record), what changed (the counters and the redline), what was taught (fourteen sessions, the packs), what came back (the OBE table, the survey beside it); Tualang reflecting in the header; the honesty line in the footer. Printed once for each judge team to keep (the booth lane).
5. **The close**: when the ring closes, Home shows Tualang celebrating for the done window as today; the booth script ends on the receipt opening in a new tab, then Home's ring, and no new claim after it (the peak-end rule).

### 9.2 The programme wall

The Courses screen gains small multiples: every course in the programme as a mini ring with its term, closed or open, and under them the programme's CLO to PLO heat table in H, M and L across the seven courses (only CS310 is filled; the others say "no outline recorded", which is true). Same data at another zoom, and it answers "would a department use it".

### 9.3 The kiosk

`#/kiosk`: between judge teams the laptop shows Tualang resting on the forest and plays the existing Loop90 render in a loop (already rendered on 22 October; nothing new is rendered), with "Touch to walk the loop"; any key or touch opens the judge's page; thirty seconds idle returns to the kiosk. WCAG 2.2.2: the film has a visible pause.

