# Research note: what a complete course record holds, how attainment is reported, and what the student surveys look like

Date: 22 October 2026, evening. For the depth plan: the sample course must read as a real course record under outcome-based education in Malaysia and internationally, the Learn step must compute attainment the way quality offices do, and the surveys must be real instruments. Compiled in a research session on 22 October 2026. Key: **P** primary (an official standard, template, syllabus or policy page); **S** secondary (a paper or guidance quoting a standard); **V** vendor. Extracted texts of the PDFs cited were saved to the session scratchpad for quoting.

## 1. What a complete course record contains

- **EAC Malaysia course files (the clearest Malaysian list).** "Course files, for every course offered by the programme, provide the course information to include the targeted course learning outcomes, a matrix linking course outcomes to programme outcomes, course synopsis/syllabus, and a list of references. Examination papers complete with answer scheme and graded examination papers with low, medium and high grades, projects, quizzes, tutorial questions, assignments, class projects, copies of the course notes. Sample of projects with low, medium and high grades. Assessment rubrics." Engineering Programme Accreditation Standard 2020, effective 1 May 2020 (UTHM mirror): https://fkaab.uthm.edu.my/docs/EAC-Standard-2020.pdf. The 2024 Standard, effective 1 January 2025, repeats the list (item viii, Appendix C): https://office.iium.edu.my/kca/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Engineering-Programme-Accreditation-Standard-2024.pdf **P**
- **MQA course assessment plan columns.** Guidelines to Good Practices: Assessment of Student Learning, 2nd edition, November 2023, Table 1: CLO, CLO statement, taxonomy domain, PLO, topics and content, contact hours, teaching and learning method, assessment methods, weightage; a Table of Specifications (2.5); Appendix 12 "Course Assessment Plan, Instruction and Rubric"; Figure 20 "Overview of the CQI Process". https://ppa.upsi.edu.my/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/DOKUMEN-GGP-AOSL-9223-FINAL-V3.pdf **P**
- **MQA curriculum map down to lessons.** Guidelines to Good Practices: Programme Design and Delivery, 2nd edition, 2023: PLOs map to CLOs and then to lesson outcomes; "each of the CLOs should be connected directly and explicitly to assessment methods and tasks"; Table 2.3 is a PLO-to-courses map for a Bachelor of Computer Science example with enabling, intermediate and culminating indicators; Table 2.4 a programme assessment plan. https://ppq.umk.edu.my/download/SURAT%20MAKLUMAN%20MQA%20BIL.%208%202023_.pdf **P**
- **A Malaysian Course Information document, 2026.** UTM training deck, 2 March 2026: seven elements: a synopsis of 100 to 150 words; CLOs with one measurable verb each, tagged Bloom C1 to C6, P1 to P7, A1 to A5; the CLO to PLO to MQF-cluster map; teaching and learning methods; assessment (continuous 50 to 70%, summative 30 to 50%); student learning time (one credit is 40 hours; a 3-credit example: 14 weeks of 3 hours of lecture, 42; 14 weeks of 1 hour of tutorial, 14; 40 hours of self-study; 12 hours of assessment preparation; 12 hours of final assessment; 120 in all); two or three references under five years old in APA. https://dvcai.utm.my/utmcaq/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2026/03/29.-Garis-Panduan-Penyediaan-Course-Information_CERAMAH-CI-FC-2.3.2026.pdf **P**
- **MQF 2nd edition, 2024.** Five clusters: Knowledge and Understanding; Cognitive Skills; Functional Work Skills (practical, interpersonal and communication, digital, numeracy, leadership); Personal and Entrepreneurial Skills; Ethics and Professionalism. One credit is 40 notional learning hours. https://office.iium.edu.my/kca/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/MQF-V2-2024.pdf **P**
- **A filled course OBE report (in Malay).** UTHM "Laporan OBE Kursus" template, 31 August 2021: CLO to PLO to learning-domain map; CLOs tagged (C4, P4, A4); CLO to teaching method, assessment method and KPI; OBE achievement from the TCIS system; grade analysis overall and per section; CLO attained or not attained with percentages; the CQI report attached. https://fptp.uthm.edu.my/images/FORM_MENU/Quality%20%20OBE/Template%20Laporan%20OBE%20Kursus%20BPB%20-%20Pindaan%20V2%202021.pdf **P** (an example, not a national template)
- **UTP itself.** The Undergraduate Student Handbook Part B (January 2026, updated August 2026) lists TFB3133 Data Visualisation (3 credits, in the Minor in Data Science): JavaScript charts, interactivity, treemaps, heat maps, network graphs, word clouds, timelines, geographic data, D3.js, a mini project; and BBB3103 Data Visualisation (business). No UTP course-outline template, CLO list or OBE handbook is public. https://www.utp.edu.my/sites/Publishing/PublishingContent/StudentHandbook/UG%20STUDENT%20HANDBOOK%20B%20-%202026%20UPDATED%20AUG%202026.pdf **P**
- **ABET.** The EAC Self-Study Questionnaire 2025 to 2026 (E002): Appendix A course syllabi, "maximum length is two pages per syllabus", with a suggested format; Criterion 4 reports assessment processes, frequency, "expected level of attainment for each of the student outcomes", result summaries and how they are documented. https://abet.org/e002_eac-self-study-questionnaire_2025-2026. The CAC criteria 2025 to 2026 give the five computing outcomes plus the computer science outcome 6. https://www.abet.org/accreditation/accreditation-criteria/criteria-for-accrediting-computing-programs-2025-2026/ **P**
- **AUN-QA version 4.0 (August 2020).** A course specification holds the title, prerequisites and credits, expected outcomes (knowledge, skills, attitude), teaching, learning and assessment methods, the outline, assessment details, and "the date on which the course specification was written or revised"; the programme specification needs "a matrix that shows how the programme learning outcomes are achieved through the courses"; requirement 4.4 asks for rubrics, marking schemes and timelines. https://natres.psu.ac.th/qa/doc/CUPT%20QA/aun2020.pdf **P** (mirror)
- **UK.** Lancaster's curriculum and assessment mapping guidance (2017, revised November 2024): programme outcomes limited to 8 to 10, modules marked Introduced, Developed or Assessed, a separate assessment map of methods and weightings. https://www.lancaster.ac.uk/media/lancaster-university/content-assets/documents/student-based-services/asq/pdda/curriculum-and-assessment-mapping/CurriculumandAssessmentMappingguidance.pdf **S**

## 2. How CLO attainment is computed and reported

- **The Malaysian KPI, direct measurement.** UTHM FKEE "OBE Practices 2024": "At least 50% of students achieve 55 marks in all CLO"; "Each PLO achieve at least 60% in average attainment"; the PLO direct measure is a CLO-to-PLO analysis "on all faculty's courses using the average approach (every semester)"; the indirect measure is a yearly graduate exit survey (a five-point Likert mean per PLO) and an employer survey; PEOs by tracer study three to five years after graduation. The OBE system's steps: define PEO, PLO and CLO; key in marks; analyse; "suggest mid/end course CQI"; report by cohort and programme. https://fkee.uthm.edu.my/images/2024/obe2024/2024%20OBE%20FKEE%20Practices.pdf **P**
- The UTHM template above applies the same rule ("50% of students reach 55% (C+) and above") and reports CLO1 84.44%, CLO2 97.04%, CLO3 98.52%. **P**
- **MQA worked targets.** GGP AOSL 2023: indirect evidence is "programme entrance and exit surveys, student interviews, alumni surveys"; example targets "60% of the students score 70 or more in a programme exit examination" and "80% of the students who indicated a score of 4 or higher on the Likert scale in a programme exit survey". **P**
- **A fully specified international formula.** UAE University "Course LOA Process": per assessment tool, attainment is the percentage of students scoring at least 70% of the tool's maximum; the CLO score is the weighted average across tools by the instructor's weights; across sections, weighted by enrolment; a projected minimum of 70%; the weighted score is actual over projected; a course effectiveness score aggregates them; an annual course assessment report. https://www.uaeu.ac.ae/en/vc/oie/pdf/clo-assessment-process.pdf **P**
- **Weighted CLO to PLO roll-up.** Derouich, "Ensuring Outcome-Based Curriculum Coherence", accepted 13 October 2025 (Discover Education; arXiv 2510.25905): question-to-CLO and CLO-to-PLO weight matrices, PLO as the weighted sum of CLOs, intended against delivered emphasis. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.25905 **S**
- **Direct and indirect disagree.** Hamid et al. (UKM), Pertanika 24, 2016: direct measures (assignment, final exam, presentation, lab report) against a pre and post survey validated with Rasch; "indirect measurement alone is not a valid measure". https://ptsldigital.ukm.my/handle/123456789/577948 **S**
- **A dashboard example.** CLOAP (BRASS Digital Lab, 1 March 2025): an R Shiny tool; the typical threshold "70% of students achieving at least 60% on each CLO", configurable; PLOs aggregated from CLOs. https://brassbe1982-bdlweb.share.connect.posit.cloud/portfolio/cloap.html **V**
- **ABET and AACSB.** ABET Criterion 4 (2025 to 2026): "regularly use appropriate, documented processes for assessing and evaluating the extent to which the student outcomes are being attained". AACSB Standard 5.1 (page updated 9 September 2025): assurance of learning "processes that include direct and indirect measures"; no numeric benchmark set. https://www.aacsb.edu/educators/accreditation/accreditation-process/ai-use-cases-for-accreditation/standard-5 **P**
- Threshold practice varies (55 marks and 50% of students at UTHM; 60 marks and 70% of students in the CLOAP default; 70% of the tool maximum at UAEU), so a product must make the threshold and the KPI configurable per institution and print them beside every attainment figure.

## 3. Student survey instruments

- **SSRT at UTP.** A UTP job advertisement (about four years old) lists "monitoring of T&L quality (e.g. Course Outcome Survey, Student Self Reflection Tools Survey (SSRT), Lecturer's Reflection)" and the use of ULearn. So SSRT is the Student Self Reflection Tools Survey, paired with a Course Outcome Survey (the per-outcome self-rating). No public instrument, scale or schedule was found. https://ng.linkedin.com/posts/universiti-teknologi-petronas_jobvacancy-utpinme-activity-6902713869836394496-fwOL **P** (thin)
- **UTP practice in 2012.** Awang, Singh and Dzulkarnain (UTP), 2012: "students' surveys are conducted at mid-semester and end of semester before the mid semester test and final examination"; ratings 1 to 7, below 5 "unsatisfactory, resulting in the lecturer being tasked to attend" training; 53 courses; a 39% correlation with class grade. https://research.utm.my/wp-content/uploads/sites/80/2013/03/1569534245.pdf **P** (may be outdated)
- **The Malaysian model instrument.** UiTM's SuFO (Rahmat et al. 2025): run each semester for every course, anonymous, 7 professionalism items plus 11 teaching and learning items on a four-point scale (strongly disagree 1 to strongly agree 4), open "two weeks prior to the study week until the formal release of the examination results". https://hrmars.com/papers_submitted/26652/lecturer-professionalism-teaching-and-learning-activities-accounting-students-perceptions.pdf **S**
- **CLO self-rating forms.** UTM's end-of-course survey rates "ability in meeting the course learning outcomes and program learning outcomes on a scale of 1 to 5" (a Scribd copy, unverified); a UPSI study used a five-point self-rating through its LMS (snippet, unverified). The GGP AOSL target "a score of 4 or higher" fits both.
- **SEEQ (Marsh).** A university-hosted form (2018): 29 items in Learning, Enthusiasm, Organization, Group Interaction, Individual Rapport, Breadth, Examinations and Assignments on a 1 to 5 scale, three overall items from Very poor to Very good, plus difficulty, workload, pace, hours a week, prior interest and expected grade. https://www.mckendree.edu/academics/vcte/resources/studentevaluationofeducationalquality.pdf **S**
- **Response norms.** Ahmad 2018 (PSU Research Review 2(3)): Nulty (2008) recommends about 58% for classes under 20 and 35% over 50; Nair et al. 2008 (Monash): 31% online against 56% on paper. https://www.emerald.com/prr/article/2/3/206/325045/Teaching-evaluation-and-student-response-rate **S**
- **Timing, anonymity and release.** Rutgers SIRS: run at the end of each semester, open "during the last week of class meetings", anonymous, "professors will not see the survey results until after they submit final grades". https://sirs.rutgers.edu/ **P**
- **Tooling and export.** Microsoft Forms "Open results in Excel" (a live link or an offline copy): https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/check-and-share-your-form-results-02859424-341d-406f-b32a-9a0fbaf357af. Google Forms "Link to Sheets" and "Download responses (.csv)": https://support.google.com/docs/answer/139706. Moodle's Feedback activity is "ideal for course or teacher evaluations", with an analysis tab and spreadsheet export (docs 5.3, 25 February 2026): https://docs.moodle.org/en/Feedback_activity. Explorance Blue (vendor): https://explorance.com/products/blue/ **P/V**

## 4. Realistic content for CS310 Data Visualisation (attribute to a template, never to UTP)

- **GWU DATS 6401 "Visualization of Complex Data", Spring 2026.** Eight outcomes (Munzner's what, why and how; encodings; Few's dashboard principles; Tableau Prep pipelines; Altair interaction; temporal, categorical and geospatial data; evaluate and iterate; present and defend). A 16-week schedule: introduction; data and task abstraction; validation; marks and channels plus Tableau; design, Gestalt, colour and accessibility; Tableau Prep; midterm; chart types and dashboard design; Altair I; break; Altair II and interaction; geospatial; temporal and uncertainty; ethics and misleading charts; storytelling. Weights: midterm 20, final 25, quizzes 15, ten in-class labs 15, a Tableau dashboard project 10 (a reproducible Prep flow, an accessibility and ethics checklist, a 5 to 10 minute defence), an Altair project 10, participation 5. Rubric emphases: task definition, data abstraction, encoding, expressiveness and effectiveness, at-a-glance principles, accessibility, uncertainty, evaluation evidence. Texts: Munzner 2014, Few 2013. https://dats.columbian.gwu.edu/syllabi/pdfs/DATS-6401-Syllabus-Spring-2026%20-%20Anya%20Mendenhall.pdf **P**
- **Other outlines.** UW-Madison CS 765, Fall 2025 (five outcomes, seven modules): https://pages.graphics.cs.wisc.edu/765-25/pages/syllabus. CCBC CSIT 251 (revised 19 October 2021; 14 objectives, 16 topics, six quizzes, six projects, two exams, a final): https://catalog.ccbcmd.edu/mime/media/47/3087/CSIT+251+10.19.21.pdf. Columbus State CSCI 2423, Spring 2026 (outcomes on visual analytics, KPIs and dashboards; a dashboard test 19%, an interactive dashboard project 28%; Tableau and Power BI): https://www.cscc.edu/academics/syllabi/pdf/CSCI%202423%20Data%20Visualization%20II%20Spring%202026.docx **P**
- **A dashboard project brief.** MSU Texas MIS 5603, Spring 2026: teams of three or four, a packaged workbook, a written report (dataset summary, cleaning steps, descriptive statistics, core charts, at least one map, one dashboard, one story, recommended actions), a 10 to 15 minute recorded presentation. https://directory.msutexas.edu//storage/files/syllabus_20260116111254.pdf **P**
- **A lab list.** Anurag Engineering College CS308PC (file dated 23 August 2024): ten Tableau labs; lab 8 "Creating Dashboards and Storytelling, adding interactivity, distributing and publishing"; a CO-PO matrix in H, M and L. https://anurag.ac.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Power-BI.pdf **P**
- **A rubric.** The BTAA Love Data Week 2026 data visualisation award guide (Northwestern): four categories scored 1 to 10: Visual Design and Presentation (appropriateness, clarity, accessibility), Clarity of Communication (narrative, context, data integrity, sources cited), Creativity and Innovation, Audience Understanding and Insight; misleading visuals (a truncated y-axis, the wrong chart type) get the lowest score. https://www.northwestern.edu/provost/about/ir/love-data/btaa-ldw-2026-data-viz-scoring-rubric.pdf **P**
- No Malaysian data-visualisation course outline with CLOs was found publicly; the UTP TFB3133 synopsis is the only local anchor.

## 5. Programme-level artefacts judges recognise

- **The courses-to-outcomes matrix.** EAC 2020 self-assessment reports must include "a matrix linking courses to POs to identify and track the contribution of each course" (a sample in Appendix G); MQA GGP PDD 2023 Table 2.3 (enabling, intermediate and culminating ticks) and Table 2.4; AUN-QA requires the same matrix; Lancaster uses Introduced, Developed and Assessed. **P** (Lancaster **S**)
- **The heatmap.** No standard mandates a heatmap; the convention is H, M and L or colour-coded levels (the Anurag CO-PO matrix; a Rajahmundry workshop report, 23 October 2025, teaching "levels of attainment (Low/Medium/High)" matrices for NAAC). Vendor reports: HelioCampus' programme course outcome report (March 2023), a PLO-to-course and CLO mapping with export. **S/V**
- **The CQI loop figure.** UTHM FKEE's flow chart: per course CLO formulation, learning and teaching activities, the lecture plan, delivery and assessment, CLO analysis, CQI at the CLO level feeding PLO analysis, then PEO analysis three to five years after graduation, with stakeholder feedback; cadence: a PLO achievement report each semester, yearly surveys and CQI, external examiner and industry advisory panel reports every one to three years. MQA GGP AOSL Figure 20 and the GGP MR-CIIQ Plan-Do-Check-Act stages with module monitoring from "summative and formative assessments, as well as through student feedback": https://office.iium.edu.my/kca/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/GGP-MR_CIIQ-BI.pdf **P**
- **Self-study structures.** ABET E002: background, Criteria 1 to 8, programme criteria, appendices A (syllabi), B (vitae), C (equipment), D (institutional summary). EAC 2020: the report by sections 7.3.1 to 7.3.9 with an Appendix C checklist and Appendix G tables 6 to 16. AUN-QA: eight criteria. The MQA document index at IIUM lists every current MQA title with dates: https://office.iium.edu.my/kca/index.php/mqa-regulatory-documents/ **P**

## What this means for Lyceum

1. The sample course needs the EAC course-file set to read as real: synopsis, five or six CLOs with Bloom tags and MQF clusters, the CLO to PLO matrix, the assessment plan with weights per CLO and a table of specifications, a 14-week schedule with student learning time that adds to 120 hours, references, lecture notes per week, lab sheets, a project brief, a rubric, exam papers with marking schemes, graded samples at low, medium and high, and the per-term OBE report with its CQI note.
2. Attainment must be computed the Malaysian way by default (the share of students at or above a mark threshold per CLO, against a KPI share) with the threshold and KPI as institution settings, the UAEU weighted variant available, direct and indirect kept apart, and the CLO to PLO roll-up as a weighted average.
3. The two surveys are the Course Outcome Survey (one item per CLO, a 1 to 5 scale, target "4 or higher") and the Student Self Reflection Tools Survey (a teaching and learning instrument adapted from SEEQ's dimensions, labelled adapted), run at the end of the semester, anonymous, with results released to the lecturer only after grades, and with response-rate norms shown beside n of N.
4. The content of the sample course is adapted from published syllabi (GWU DATS 6401 first) and says so on the course record; nothing is attributed to UTP's own course.
5. Programme-level views judges recognise: the courses-to-PLO matrix with levels, the CQI loop, and the one-page course OBE report.


===== FILE: docs/research/before-and-after-patterns-2026-10-22.md =====
# Research note: showing before and after

Date: 22 October 2026, evening. For the depth plan: how products show what changed between two versions of a document, a slide, a table or a result, how curriculum systems present proposed changes to committees, and which patterns are honest and accessible. Compiled in a research session on 22 October 2026; dates are page dates where shown.

## 1. Document and content diff UX

- **GitHub** renders prose diffs as source or rendered; rendered diffs highlight added and removed text and show invisible changes (a link target) in a tooltip; large diffs fall back to source. Images compare in 2-up, swipe (a slider) and onion skin (opacity). https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/working-with-files/using-files/working-with-non-code-files
- **Google Docs Compare** (11 June 2019) writes a third document in which every difference is a suggested edit attributed to a chosen name; the originals are untouched. https://workspaceupdates.googleblog.com/2019/06/compare-docs.html
- **Word legal blackline**: a third document with differences as tracked changes at word or character level; moves, formatting, case, white space and tables are all compared by default. https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/compare-document-differences-using-the-legal-blackline-option-dbfc7351-4022-43a2-a0c4-54d1898702a0
- **Notion snapshot diffs** (26 May 2026): choose two snapshots and it highlights additions, deletions and modifications across text, images, tables and database properties. https://www.createwith.com/tool/notion/updates/notion-adds-snapshot-diffs-to-version-history-for-easier-change-tracking
- **Figma branch review**: changed layers listed per page; side by side by default, or an overlay with an opacity toggle. https://help.figma.com/hc/en-us/articles/5691189138839
- **Confluence** (page modified 9 October 2026): tick two versions, compare; green added, red deleted, blue formatting; unchanged blocks collapse. https://confluence.atlassian.com/doc/page-history-and-page-comparison-views-139379.html
- **Slide tools**: PowerPoint Compare merges two decks into a Revisions pane split into slide changes and presentation changes, accepted by checkbox (https://edu.gcfglobal.org/en/powerpoint/reviewing-presentations/1); Pitch keeps per-slide history on a timeline and restores a slide as a new slide (https://pitch.com/whats-new/version-history-and-deleted-slide-recovery); Gamma restores whole versions and users complain it shows "that something changed, but not what" (https://ideas.gamma.app/ideas/p/improve-version-history). None diffs slide content structurally.

## 2. Visualising change in structured data

- **Slopegraph** (storytelling with data, 27 July 2020): two points per line, "the steeper the slope, the bigger the change"; unrelated categories become "piles of dried spaghetti"; use bars when exact values matter. https://www.storytellingwithdata.com/blog/2020/7/27/what-is-a-slopegraph
- **Arrow and range (dumbbell) plots** (Datawrapper, 22 September 2017): an arrow plot "to emphasize the change between values more than the values themselves"; a range plot when the gap matters. https://www.datawrapper.de/blog/new-arrow-and-range-plots
- **FT Visual Vocabulary**: slope is "good for showing changing data as long as the data can be simplified into 2 or 3 points"; paired columns "can become tricky to read with more than 2 series". https://github.com/Financial-Times/chart-doctor/blob/main/visual-vocabulary/README.md
- **Juxtaposition, superposition, explicit encoding** (Gleicher et al., Information Visualization, 2011): every comparison design is one of the three; juxtaposition relies on memory, superposition on the visual system, explicit encoding computes and shows the difference. https://doi.org/10.1177/1473871611416549
- **Animated transitions** (Heer and Robertson, 2007): animation "can significantly improve graphical perception"; staged beats unstaged; avoid rescaling axes mid-transition. https://idl.uw.edu/papers/animated-transitions. **Object constancy** (Bostock, 16 May 2012): key marks by identity so a bar can be tracked through the transition. https://bost.ocks.org/mike/constancy/
- **Accessibility**: WCAG 2.2.2 (Level A) requires pause, stop or hide for auto-playing motion over five seconds (https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/pause-stop-hide.html); 2.3.3 with techniques C39 and SCR40 for prefers-reduced-motion in CSS and JS; Chartability (2022) adds vestibular and cognitive heuristics for charts (https://diglib.eg.org/handle/10.1111/cgf14522).

## 3. Curriculum change presentation in universities

- **CourseLeaf CIM**: "Red strike-through text is used to indicate deletions and green text is used to indicate additions" (Penn State, https://cim.psu.edu/user-guides/course-management/edit-course/); additions are green with a double underline at Ohio State; approvers filter by user, "All changes" or "Hide changes", and print follows the filter (Montana State, June 2025, https://www.montana.edu/provost/curriculum-development/approving_new_course_or_program_using_cim.html).
- **Curriculog user tracking** (UMBC manual, 2021): "Show current", "Show original", "Show current with markup", per-user colour, who and when. https://gradschool.umbc.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/155/2022/07/Curriculog-User-Manual-2021.pdf
- **Kuali CM**: the approver view shows "requested changes (yellow)" against "existing course information (blue)". https://www.csupueblo.edu/kuali-student-curriculum-management/_doc/course-training-power-point.pdf
- **Coursedog**: "View Changes" shows where new content replaces the original (https://support.stevens.edu/support/solutions/articles/19000138726); Arizona's form pairs "Current Requirements" on the left with "New Requirements" on the right (February 2026, https://curricularaffairs.arizona.edu/sites/default/files/2026-02/Guide-to-coursedog-substantial-change.pdf).
- **Committee forms**: "Current Curriculum" and "Proposed Curriculum" columns by semester (LSU Eunice Form F, https://www.lsue.edu/academic-affairs/cdmdocs/CCFormF2.pdf); Western University's minor-modification template (January 2025) puts existing text beside the change; ETSU pairs a current and a proposed narrative per change (https://www.etsu.edu/chairshandbook/documents/august_06_13_substantialcoursemodification.pdf).

## 4. Timeline and scrubber interfaces

- **Figma**: a checkpoint every 30 minutes, autosaves grouped and expandable, named versions carry a title and a description, restore is non-destructive. https://help.figma.com/hc/en-us/articles/360038006754
- **Webflow backups**: a restore point every 50th autosave; an eye icon previews a backup before restoring; a restore saves the current state first. https://help.webflow.com/hc/en-us/articles/33961244069395
- **GitHub blame**: per-line commit blocks and "View blame prior to this change" to step back one revision. https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/working-with-files/using-files/viewing-and-understanding-files
- **Perforce Time-lapse View**: a slider by date, changelist or revision, an "aging" shade where darker is more recent, action badges under the slider. https://www.perforce.com/manuals/p4v/Content/P4V/advanced_files.timelapse.html
- **Keynote**: "Browse All Versions" shows a tick-marked timeline with thumbnails of each saved version. https://support.apple.com/guide/keynote/tan7f1de6ec5/mac
- Legibility across all of them: name the stop you are on, show who and when on hover, keep unchanged content still.

## 5. Slide-level change

- **PowerPoint Compare**: the Revisions pane lists slide changes and presentation changes; selecting one highlights it on the slide. https://edu.gcfglobal.org/en/powerpoint/reviewing-presentations/1
- **Google Slides**: version history with a "Show changes" box that highlights edits in green, one colour per editor; up to 40 named versions. https://support.google.com/docs/answer/190843
- **Two versions as images**: pptx-glimpse renders PPTX to SVG or PNG in Node 22 or the browser (https://www.npmjs.com/package/pptx-glimpse); LibreOffice headless to PDF then pdftoppm; pixelmatch returns a diff image and a mismatch count (https://github.com/mapbox/pixelmatch). Since Lyceum generates packs from records, render the slide model to the DOM or SVG for both versions and diff the record, not the pixels.

## 6. "Wow" patterns for change

- **Before and after slider**: Knight Lab JuxtaposeJS, for "then and now" stories, with "your audience control over the slider". https://juxtapose.knightlab.com/
- **Slider accessibility**: drag-only sliders fail WCAG 2.5.7 Dragging Movements and 2.1.1; fix with arrow keys, click-to-position, an accessible name and value, or plain "Show before" and "Show after" buttons; alt text should describe the difference. https://dev.to/agentkit/before-and-after-image-sliders-the-drag-only-widget-that-fails-wcag-22-1759. Reuters Graphics ships a keyboard-accessible BeforeAfter component: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@reuters-graphics/graphics-components@4.7.0/dist/llm-docs/components/BeforeAfter.md
- **Morphing**: the View Transitions API captures the old and new DOM and animates between them, "reducing users' cognitive load, helping them stay in context"; MDN warns about focus and live-region confusion. https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/API/View_Transition_API. Keynote's Magic Move moves objects "from their positions on one slide to new positions on the next". https://support.apple.com/kb/ph16958
- **Why reveals land**: Duarte's sparkline alternates "what is" with "what could be" (https://tomtunguz.com/storytelling-duarte/); NN/g (12 January 2020): motion gets feedback and state changes noticed, irrelevant motion "can substantially degrade the user experience" (https://www.nngroup.com/articles/animation-purpose-ux/); NN/g on change blindness: users miss changes far from their focus (https://www.nngroup.com/articles/change-blindness-definition/).
- **Counters**: no independent evidence that count-up animations persuade; only vendor guidance (test it). https://documentation.unbounce.com/hc/en-us/articles/35430836874900--Tips-Scripts-Count-Up-Animation

## Ten patterns for Lyceum

1. **Word-level redline** for outcome wording and assessment item stems: red strikethrough and green insertion inside one paragraph, the curriculum-system convention, with a "hide changes" toggle for the clean proposed text. Never colour alone: keep the strikethrough and underline and add visually hidden "deleted" and "inserted" labels.
2. **Current versus proposed, two columns** for topics and hours and for assessment items: the committee convention, the changed cell highlighted, an explicit delta column such as "+2 h". A real table with column headers; the delta as text, not only an arrow.
3. **A dumbbell per topic** for hours: the before dot, the after dot, a connecting bar, sorted by the size of change; an arrow plot when direction is the story. Value labels on both dots and a data table beneath.
4. **A slopegraph for cohort results**: one line per outcome from last semester to this, the chosen line emphasised and the rest grey. Every line end labelled in text; grey lines at 3:1 contrast.
5. **Swipe and onion-skin slide compare**: both versions rendered to images, a divider the judge drags or an opacity fade, GitHub-style. Arrow keys, click-to-position and "Show before" and "Show after" buttons (WCAG 2.5.7).
6. **Structural slide diff**: because packs come from records, list what changed (title, bullet, image, table cell) as a Revisions-style list that highlights the region on the thumbnail; the list is the accessible description, so no one depends on pixels.
7. **A version scrubber with named stops** in History: grouped autosave ticks and labelled named versions, who and when on hover, the diff always against the previous stop. A slider role with keyboard steps and an announced stop name; no autoplay without a pause control.
8. **Object-constant animated transition** for hours and results: bars animate from before to after keyed by topic id, staged so values move while axes stay fixed; with reduced motion, jump to the end state with the delta in text.
9. **Change-summary counters**: a header such as "3 outcomes reworded, 4 h moved, 2 slides revised" computed from the diff, counting up once; final numbers rendered immediately for reduced-motion users and in a live region.
10. **The "what is, what could be" demo arc**: hold the old state for a few seconds, reveal the new with one pattern above, and leave the comparison on screen; the persisted comparison, not the motion, carries the information.


===== FILE: docs/research/slides-items-and-surveys-tech-2026-10-22.md =====
# Research note: branded decks, assessment items and surveys without a server

Date: 22 October 2026, evening. For the depth plan: what pptxgenjs can and cannot do for a branded, editable, accessible deck; how to preview slides in the browser; the item and rubric formats an LMS accepts; and how a local-first app can collect student survey responses. Compiled in a research session on 22 October 2026; vendor pages marked **V**, unverified items marked.

## 1. pptxgenjs capabilities and limits

- The current release is 4.0.1 (26 June 2025); 4.0.0 (4 May 2025) added text direction and fixed master reuse. MIT. https://github.com/gitbrent/PptxGenJS/releases
- **Masters**: `defineSlideMaster({ title, background, margin, objects: [rect, line, text, image, placeholder], slideNumber })`; placeholders are named objects of type title, body, image, chart, table or media, filled with `addText(..., { placeholder: "body" })`; masters become editable layouts under View, Slide Master. https://gitbrent.github.io/PptxGenJS/docs/masters/
- **Images**: PNG, JPG and GIF; SVG "supported in the newest desktop PowerPoint or Microsoft 365" with no documented fallback, so ship a PNG raster of the institution's logo and the SVG only as an extra; sizing contain, cover and crop; image hyperlinks. https://gitbrent.github.io/PptxGenJS/docs/api-images/
- **Charts**: area, bar, bar3d, bubble, doughnut, line, pie, radar, scatter and combo as native chart XML with an embedded workbook. https://gitbrent.github.io/PptxGenJS/docs/api-charts/
- **Tables**: colspan and rowspan, per-cell formatting, auto-paging with a repeated header ("not an exact science"). https://gitbrent.github.io/PptxGenJS/docs/api-tables/
- Notes with `slide.addNotes()`, sections with `pptx.addSection()`, hyperlinks to a URL or a slide, `fit: shrink` or `resize`, bullets with indent levels. https://gitbrent.github.io/PptxGenJS/docs/speaker-notes/
- **Theme**: only head and body font faces and language; no theme colour palette API, but scheme colours are accepted in colour and fill. Layouts 16:9 (10 by 5.625 inches), wide, or custom. https://gitbrent.github.io/PptxGenJS/docs/usage-pres-options/
- **Limits**: no font embedding; animations are not on the roadmap; it cannot edit an existing PPTX.
- **Known defects**, issue 1449 (opened 31 March 2026, still reproducing on 4.0.1 in September): string shape presets such as `"oval"` and `"roundedRectangle"` write invalid XML (use the `pres.shapes` constants), one slide-master content-types override per slide, a malformed notes master; the issue carries a post-process repair function. https://github.com/gitbrent/PptxGenJS/issues/1449
- **Thumbnails without PowerPoint**: LibreOffice headless to PDF then pdftoppm (https://help.libreoffice.org/latest/en-US/text/shared/guide/start_parameters.html); pptx-glimpse renders PPTX to SVG or PNG in Node 22 or the browser, "not pixel-identical" (https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pptx-glimpse/README.md); pptx-browser 4.1.5 draws on a canvas (https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pptx-browser@4.1.5/README.md); Microsoft Graph converts to PDF or JPG with a 2 MB cap and a sign-in (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/driveitem-get-content-format) **V**. Cheapest and most faithful for Lyceum: render Lyceum's own slide spec to SVG in React, and rasterise with Puppeteer only for export.

## 2. Alternatives for slide generation

- python-pptx (MIT, 1.0.0): placeholders, charts, tables, notes; no rendering. https://python-pptx.readthedocs.io/en/latest/
- Marp CLI (MIT): `--pptx` exports images plus notes; `--pptx-editable` is experimental and needs a browser and LibreOffice Impress, with "lower slide reproducibility" and no notes. https://github.com/marp-team/marp-cli/blob/main/README.md
- Slidev (MIT): PPTX as images by default; an editable mode rebuilds native shapes but SVG, maths and gradients stay pictures. https://sli.dev/guide/exporting
- reveal.js (MIT): PDF through the browser or decktape; no PPTX. https://revealjs.com/pdf-export/
- Google Slides API: `batchUpdate` creates native slides from layouts with OAuth; PPTX through Drive export. https://developers.google.com/workspace/slides/api/guides/overview **V**
- Microsoft Graph has no PowerPoint content API. An Office Add-in can call `PowerPoint.Presentation.insertSlidesFromBase64(base64, { formatting: "UseDestinationTheme" })` (PowerPointApi 1.2), so a pptxgenjs deck can be dropped into a university's own template inside PowerPoint. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/javascript/api/powerpoint/powerpoint.presentation **V**
- Editable text comes from pptxgenjs, python-pptx, the Slides API and Office.js; Marp and Slidev defaults and reveal.js give images only.

## 3. Assessment item formats and generation

- **QTI 3.0**, final in May 2022 (https://www.1edtech.org/standards/qti/versions). From the best-practice guide: a multiple-choice item declares a single-cardinality identifier response, a correct response, a float SCORE outcome, a choice interaction with one max choice and simple choices, and the match-correct response-processing template; short answer uses a text-entry interaction (expected length, pattern mask, placeholder); essay uses an extended-text interaction (expected lines, min and max strings, plain, preformatted or xhtml) with no automatic scoring; a rubric block takes a view (author, candidate, scorer and so on); a package is a zip with an IMS manifest. https://www.imsglobal.org/spec/qti/v3p0/impl
- **What the LMSs import**: Canvas New Quizzes imports QTI 1.2 and 2.x (https://community.canvaslms.com/docs/DOC-26303); Blackboard Ultra banks import QTI 2.1 for true or false, multiple choice, multiple answer, fill in the blank and essay (July 2025) **V**; Moodle imports Moodle XML, GIFT and Aiken, not QTI 3.
- **Moodle XML** (https://docs.moodle.org/en/Moodle_XML_format, checked against Moodle 4.5 source): `<question type="multichoice|shortanswer|essay">`, `<name><text>`, `<questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[...]]>`, `<answer fraction="100"><text>` with `<feedback>`; multichoice has `<single>`, `<shuffleanswers>`, `<answernumbering>` and the three feedback elements; shortanswer `<usecase>`; essay `<responseformat>`, `<responserequired>`, `<responsefieldlines>`, word limits, attachments, `<graderinfo>`, `<responsetemplate>`; UTF-8 with the XML declaration alone on line 1.
- **Bloom verbs** (University of Arkansas, 26 July 2022, CC BY-NC-ND): Remember list, define, name, label, recall; Understand describe, explain, paraphrase, summarise, interpret; Apply calculate, predict, solve, demonstrate, use; Analyse classify, categorise, diagram, break down; Evaluate judge, defend, justify, argue; Create design, formulate, compose, develop. https://tips.uark.edu/using-blooms-taxonomy/
- **Item-writing rules**: Haladyna, Downing and Rodriguez 2002 (Applied Measurement in Education 15(3)): 31 guidelines in five groups (content, format, style, the stem, the choices). https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ660246. The rules a deterministic generator can enforce: a positive stem that carries the main idea; three options suffice; one keyed answer in varied positions; independent, homogeneous options of equal length; no "all of the above" or "none of the above"; distractors from common errors; no absolutes or grammar clues; no trick items. https://cpl.health.unm.edu/AssetListing/Writing-Questions-for-Learning-and-Assessment-JiTL-2573/Rules-for-Multiple-Choice-Items-Haladyna-et-al-2002-5943
- **Rubrics**: the AAC&U VALUE rubrics (2009; CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, attribution required, commercial use needs permission, adaptations share alike). https://www.aacu.org/value/how-to-cite. Ship the four-level grid shape (capstone, two milestones, benchmark) with lecturer-written descriptors, and link rather than bundle the AAC&U text.

## 4. Survey collection without a server

- **Microsoft Forms** has no create API ("Creating forms directly through an API in Microsoft Forms isn't supported", Microsoft Q&A, 3 January 2025, https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/2139869/creating-microsoft-forms-via-graph-api-in-power-au); Quick Import builds a form from a Word or PDF file (10 MB, local file only; matching and multi-blank items become open text). https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/forms/convert-a-word-or-pdf-form-or-quiz-to-microsoft-forms **V**. **Google Forms API**: `forms.create` plus `batchUpdate` with OAuth, 375 writes a minute per project; forms created by the API after 30 June 2026 start unpublished. https://developers.google.com/workspace/forms/api/limits **V**
- **The form in the URL**: lz-string `compressToEncodedURIComponent` (MIT, https://github.com/pieroxy/lz-string); a fragment never reaches a server; keep links under about 2,000 characters. Answers return as a short base64 token the student pastes into the LMS or an email.
- **QR to a static page with a collector**: the qrcode npm package (MIT; byte mode holds 2,953 characters at level L, https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/qrcode/README.md); Formspree free tier 50 submissions a month, cross-origin AJAX, a 30-day archive (https://formspree.io/plans/) **V**; Basin free tier one form, 50 a month **V**; a Google Apps Script web app posting into a Sheet (6 minutes per execution, https://developers.google.com/apps-script/guides/services/quotas) **V**; Cloudflare Workers free tier 100,000 requests a day with KV at 100,000 reads and 1,000 writes a day (https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/, https://developers.cloudflare.com/kv/platform/limits/) **V**.
- **Imports**: Microsoft Forms Excel exports ID, Start time, Completion time, Email, Name, then one column per question, with Name and Email blank when anonymous (https://support.microsoft.com/topic/02859424-341d-406f-b32a-9a0fbaf357af) **V**; Google Forms exports Timestamp first, Email Address only if collected, question text as headers, CSV unsorted, timestamp text locale-dependent (https://support.google.com/docs/answer/139706); Qualtrics exports CSV, TSV, XLSX and SPSS **V**; Moodle Feedback exports CSV, XLSX, HTML, JSON and ODS (https://docs.moodle.org/405/en/Data_formats).
- **The tools universities use**: Microsoft Forms inside the Microsoft 365 tenant, Google Forms, Qualtrics, EvaSys, LMS surveys. UTP runs two Moodle instances (ULearn and ULearnX) integrated with Microsoft 365 (a Pukunui case study, undated, https://pukunui.com.my/universiti-teknologi-petronas/) **V**; https://ucs.utp.edu.my/knowledgebase/article/KA-01294/en-us

## 5. Anonymity and consent

- McGill (Senate, 23 April 2014): a minimum of five responses for classes of 5 to 11, then 40, 35, 30 and 25 percent by size; nothing disclosed to the instructor before final grades are processed; comments confidential to the instructor and the unit head. https://www.mcgill.ca/mercury/about/policy
- McLennan: five per section, results after grades (https://mclennan.edu/data/Course%20Evaluations/evaluation-faqs.html); UNC Charlotte: three (https://myevals.charlotte.edu/faqs/); Ruhr University Bochum: reports from ten cases, raw free text excluded unless categorised (February 2024).
- GDPR: Edinburgh Napier relies on the public task basis and warns that confidentiality holds "unless students identify themselves in free-text comments" (https://documentcentre.napier.ac.uk/-/media/files/university-governance/privacy-notice/privacynoticemoduleevaluation.pdf); Göttingen pseudonymises with transaction numbers.
- Malaysia: PDPA 2010 as amended in 2024, phased in during 2025 (a data protection officer, breach notification, fines up to RM 1 million); section 3 exempts the federal and state governments and the coverage of public universities is unsettled (https://amcham.com.my/malaysian-personal-data-protection-act-2010-does-it-apply-to-government-agencies/). UTP is run by a private company, whose privacy notice for students (updated June 2025) applies: https://www.utp.edu.my/sites/Publishing/PublishingContent/privacy-notice-general.pdf

## 6. Institutional slide templates

- Bath: one template for all staff, 16:9, full-screen intro, title variants, content slides, "designed to meet accessibility requirements" (https://www.bath.ac.uk/guides/using-university-branded-templates/); Manchester: the logo top-left on every slide, three background families (https://www.staffnet.manchester.ac.uk/brand/visual-identity/guidelines/presentations/); Yale: a brand typeface with a system-font fallback deck (https://yaleidentity.yale.edu/guidelines/presentations); CMU: three templates in Open Sans and Source Serif Pro (https://brand.cmu.edu/resources/downloads). The pattern: a title slide (logo, title, course, lecturer), section headers, content slides, a closing slide; a footer of course code, week and slide number is lecturer practice rather than a brand rule.
- UTP publishes no brand kit; its site describes the logo (a floral bud with the Twin Towers outline, in gold and blue). The owner's SVG is the source of truth and its hex values (#004A84 blue, #BC893A gold) come from it. https://utp.edu.my/Pages/Students/Current/Academic%20Regulations%20and%20Procedure/General-Information.aspx
- **Accessibility Checker rules for PowerPoint**: errors are missing alt text, a slide without a title, default section names and restricted access; warnings are table structure, text contrast, captions and reading order; tips are unique section names and slide titles (https://support.microsoft.com/en-ie/office/rules-for-the-accessibility-checker-651e08f2-0fc3-4e10-aaca-74b4a67101c1) **V**. Microsoft's guidance: 18 pt or larger, a sans serif, unique titles, built-in layouts for reading order, descriptive links, no colour-only meaning. WCAG2ICT (W3C Group Note, 11 December 2025) maps WCAG 2.x to non-web documents: https://www.w3.org/TR/wcag2ict-22/

## Recommended design for Lyceum

1. **Decks**: keep pptxgenjs 4.0.1; a `Brand` record (a palette, head and body fonts, the logo as a PNG rasterised from the owner's SVG, the SVG as an extra) drives five slide masters (title, section, content, two-column, closing) with named placeholders; notes on every slide; a section per week; every slide titled; objects added in reading order; body text 18 pt or larger.
2. **Deck preview**: render the same slide spec to SVG in React (no PowerPoint needed); pptx-glimpse in Node as the thumbnail and regression renderer; LibreOffice-to-PDF only in a check; shapes through the `pres.shapes` constants plus the issue-1449 repair pass.
3. **Deck fallback**: an Office Add-in calling `insertSlidesFromBase64` with the destination theme when a university supplies its own template; the Google Slides API for Workspace campuses. Both planned.
4. **Items**: deterministic templates keyed by Bloom level and item type, using the Arkansas verb list and the course's outcome records; a Haladyna lint (three or four options, equal length, a positive stem, no all-or-none options, distractors from recorded misconceptions).
5. **Item export**: Moodle XML first (UTP's ULearn is Moodle), a QTI 2.1 package second (Canvas, Blackboard Ultra), QTI 3.0 third, all from one item model; rubrics as a four-level grid exported to DOCX and XLSX and as a QTI rubric block for scorers.
6. **Surveys**: survey definitions as JSON in the workspace with two templates (the per-outcome self-rating on 1 to 5, the teaching survey); every question carries a stable id embedded in its text so imports match.
7. **Collection, primary**: a DOCX export shaped for Microsoft Forms Quick Import (Microsoft 365 campuses) and Google Forms through its API when signed in.
8. **Collection, fallback**: a static responder page on the public site, opened by QR, with the form in the URL fragment through lz-string, posting to a Cloudflare Worker with KV (the free tier covers a course) or Formspree; the last resort a paste-this-code token.
9. **Results back**: importers for Microsoft Forms XLSX, Google Forms CSV, Moodle Feedback CSV and XLSX, and Qualtrics CSV through SheetJS, matched on the question id; aggregates only at five responses or more; free text locked until the lecturer marks grades released; a consent line and the privacy notice on every form; no identifiers stored.
10. **Verification**: golden-file tests per writer (PPTX XML diff, a Moodle XML import into a local Moodle, QTI validated against the 1EdTech schema) beside the existing checks.


===== FILE: docs/research/judge-experience-and-wow-2026-10-22.md =====
# Research note: the judge's experience, and honest wow

Date: 22 October 2026, evening. For the depth plan: guided tours and kiosk modes, what wins education hackathon demos, booth practice for a judged ten minutes, which wow mechanics are honest, how a mascot may appear before a professional audience, and how to run a public demo site. Compiled in a research session on 22 October 2026; vendor pages marked **V**, unverified items marked.

## Format check

The official site (hackastone.varteledu.com, read 22 October 2026) announces the Gold, Silver and Bronze awards on 30 October 2026 at the Holland Conference on AI for Education, VU Amsterdam. ITS's report on the finalist EduVent (9 October 2026, https://www.its.ac.id/?p=72149) describes a 10-minute presentation, a 5-minute Q&A and a live demonstration, not 8 plus 2. The finalist pack settles the timings; no rubric was found.

## 1. Guided tours and self-guided demo modes

- driver.js: MIT, about 5 kB, no dependencies, tours, hints, hooks, React support (https://driverjs.com/) **V**. Shepherd.js is AGPL-3.0 or commercial from version 14; Intro.js is also AGPL or commercial; react-joyride is MIT (3.1.0, React 16.8 to 19).
- Navattic's 2026 report on 40,000 demos (**V**, https://www.navattic.com/report/state-of-the-interactive-product-demo-2026): the top 1% of demos hold viewers 2.2 minutes; 5 to 13 steps per flow; a first step of 10 to 20 words; completion 57% for multi-flow demos against 35%; ungated beats gated. Arcade's benchmarks (16 May 2025, **V**): the top 1% use nine steps; an opening question "which use case?" lifts the play rate 72%.
- Kiosk: Chrome's kiosk flag has no idle reset; add an idle-reset extension and a restricted OS account (https://github.com/substantial/kiosk-manager-chrome).
- A character guiding a tour: Duo welcomes, then asks for a goal before sign-up (https://www.reallygoodux.io/blog/duolingo-user-onboarding); Notion's corner figure asks setup questions. Clippy failed on mistimed interruptions and on being "optimised for first use" (https://www2.cio.co.nz/article/662045/what-went-wrong-clippy-virtual-assistant-pioneer-people-loved-hate/).

## 2. What wins education and AI hackathon demos

- A judge's notes (Magomed Kurbaitaev, 27 July, year not shown): a live working demo beats a more ambitious project on slides "almost every time"; say what it is in the first sentence; a named weakness raised scores; keep a 60-second screen capture as insurance; "pick the sentence you want a judge to repeat to another judge an hour later". https://dev.to/kurbaitaev/what-judges-actually-score-notes-from-a-year-of-hackathon-judging-3p4l
- Wharton Hack-AI-thon, 29 May 2025: the winner (handwriting flags dyslexia, lesson plans drafted) was praised for the clear explanation of its technical reasoning. https://ai-analytics.wharton.upenn.edu/news/fighting-dyslexia-breaking-chatbots-inside-the-winning-projects-of-whartons-2025-hack-ai-thon/
- Imagine Cup: finals are live before a judge panel; semifinals allow a live or pre-recorded demo with live narration. https://imaginecup.microsoft.com/en-us/competition/17897. Google Solution Challenge 2024: the top ten presented live on a demo day. Devpost events cap demo videos at two minutes; one education hackathon judges "a believable before-and-after" (search snippet only, unverified).

## 3. Booth design for a judged ten minutes

- A packing list (EdWeek MarketBrief, 7 May 2015): a sign readable across the room, a monitor on a stand so visitors do not bend over a laptop, a phone hotspot because expo wifi is overloaded; a one-person booth looks harder to approach. https://marketbrief.edweek.org/the-startup-blog/demoing_your_startup_at_an_expo_the_ultimate_packing_list/
- A drop-in booth pattern (Wikimedia, edited March 2026): one short demo ready, non-public internet, leaflets to take away. https://meta.wikimedia.org/wiki/Learning_patterns/Drop-in_demo_booth
- Judges in sequence (UCI showcase tips, 2025): judges rotate independently, 5 to 15 minutes each, let them physically engage, bring multiple units. https://bbcomp.tech.uci.edu/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/BB-Comp-Showcase-Tips-and-Tricks-2025.pdf. A science-fair tip sheet: prepare a 1 to 2 minute and a 5 to 7 minute version. https://stem.idaho.gov/wp-content/uploads/IDSEF/ISEF-Science-Fair-Competition-Tips.pdf
- A memorable booth: TAR UMT's Gold Best Booth at PAASWE, 8 to 12 October 2025; judges noticed a practical bench component. https://eduadvisor.my/articles/tar-umt-paaswe-2025

## 4. Honest wow mechanics

- Count-up counters: only vendor guidance (trigger once, real figures). Precedent: Shopify's Live Globe shows orders per minute (2024, https://www.shopify.com/news/live-globe-2024).
- Live links as receipts: an AAAI 2025 study found citations raised trust even when random, but users who checked them trusted less, so the link must survive checking. https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.01303
- Before and after slider: Knight Lab JuxtaposeJS, 24 September 2014, built so journalists "can actually show change". https://knightlab.northwestern.edu/2014/09/24/introducing-juxtaposejs-an-easy-way-to-compare-two-frames/index.html
- A one-click artefact: Microsoft's Copilot event of 16 March 2023 turned a Word file into a deck; the press caveat: "the demos looked wonderful, but how things work out when exposed to real users is an entirely different manner". https://office-watch.com/2023/sneak-peek-at-microsoft-copilot/
- A side-by-side assistant: the MCP launch, 25 November 2024, shipped local servers in Claude Desktop. https://www.anthropic.com/news/model-context-protocol
- A printed receipt: a GitHub-issue receipt printer (The Register, 29 March 2022, https://www.theregister.com/2022/03/29/github_issue_printer/); BERG's Little Printer: paper is "a screen that never turns off".
- A term recap: Wrapped works on self-knowledge, self-expression and comparison. https://adma.com.au/resources/personal-shareable-no-strings-attached-the-winning-formula-behind-spotify-wrapped
- A programme view: small multiples, "the best design solution" for a wide range of problems (Tufte, via Pew, 20 December 2018). https://www.pewresearch.org/decoded/2018/12/20/how-pew-research-center-uses-small-multiple-charts/
- Peak-end: Kahneman et al. 1993; Redelmeier, Katz and Kahneman 2003; Kemp 2008 calls it "not an outstandingly good predictor". Picture superiority: Standing 1973, recognition of 10,000 pictures "almost limitless" (https://www.sas.upenn.edu/psych/rust-lab/publications/standing_73.pdf); Medina's 10% against 65% figure has no primary citation (unverified).

## 5. Mascot presence for judges

- GitHub's brand rules (read 22 October 2026): mascots fit community, celebrations and education "as long as they aren't doing the teaching"; "Don't use mascots to explain, interrupt, or sell"; apologies and crises need proper copy. https://brand.github.com/graphic-elements/mascots
- Mailchimp: Freddie "smiles, winks, and sometimes high-fives, but he does not talk"; "If you're unsure, keep a straight face". https://styleguide.mailchimp.com/voice-and-tone/
- Duolingo: Duo "mostly stands still, and doesn't talk or make sounds", reserved for excitement and special occasions (the design page now redirects; unverified).
- Atlassian: the wink only "once you've built trust"; "Delight means little flourishes, not humor"; "Once may amuse, but a dozen times may annoy". https://atlassian.design/foundations/content/voice-tone. Google: never humorous error messages. https://developers.google.com/tech-writing/error-messages/set-tone
- Headspace's 2024 rebrand kept its characters but added photography for "a more professional persona". https://www.itsnicethat.com/articles/italic-studio-headspace-graphic-design-project-250424
- The rule for Tualang: speaks at welcome, empty states, step completions and the term recap; silent on errors, constraint failures, data provenance and pricing.

## 6. Live public demo sites

- Plausible's demo is its own live traffic, no login (https://plausible.io/plausible.io). Grafana Play disabled anonymous dashboard creation for security on 15 January 2025. Omeka's sandbox resets four times a week.
- Safety: a demo-mode flag that disables destructive operations and caps sizes; Vercel WAF rate-limit rules need a follow-up deny or 429 (https://vercel.com/docs/vercel-firewall/vercel-waf/rate-limiting); Stripe keeps a test-data banner on screen (https://docs.stripe.com/test-mode).
- QR: width about one tenth of the viewing distance, 6 to 10 cm on an A1 poster, a quiet zone of four modules, the typed URL printed under it, tested on iPhone and Android (**V**: https://www.qr-code-generator.com/blog/qr-code-distance/).

## Ranked wow moments for Lyceum

1. **The live receipt**: the judge clicks a quotation and the employer's posting opens with the sentence highlighted. Honest because the link is real and must survive checking; small.
2. **Tualang's two-minute phone tour**: the judge scans the poster and Tualang walks 9 to 12 steps through the workspace, labelled synthetic on every screen. Honest because the banner never leaves; medium.
3. **The before and after outline slider**: a Juxtapose-style handle between last term's outline and this term's, each change linked to its decision record. Honest because every diff has a recorded reason; medium.
4. **The one-click pack**: a PPTX downloads and opens in PowerPoint, editable. Honest because it is the real generator's output; small, built.
5. **The same question, two doors**: Lyceum's Report beside Claude Desktop answering through the read-only MCP server. Honest because both read one exported file the judge can open; medium.
6. **Count-up to the claims register**: postings, quotations and decisions count up once, with the register beside them. Honest because every figure is already audited; small.
7. **A printed receipt**: a thermal printer prints the quotation, URL, date and a QR code while the judge watches. Honest because it prints a stored record; medium, needs hardware.
8. **The term recap page**: one A4 per semester in a Wrapped style, printed for the judge to keep. Honest because each number traces to a record; medium.
9. **The programme wall**: small multiples of every course in a programme, each a mini loop ring. Honest because it is the same data at another zoom; medium.
10. **A kiosk attract loop**: between judge teams the laptop plays the 60-second real capture and resets on touch. Honest because the capture is of the real app; small.
11. **The named weak spot**: Tualang holds a card listing what is not built, read aloud. Honest by definition, and judges reward it; small.
12. **Decision replay**: scrub a timeline to watch one decision form from quotation to outline change on real timestamps. Honest because it animates stored events only; large.
13. **The judge as lecturer**: the judge names a skill and Watch finds a real posting live, with the recorded fallback one key away. Honest because the fallback is labelled recorded; medium.
14. **A second screen**: a monitor on a stand mirrors the laptop so three judges see without bending. Small.
15. **The peak-end close**: pitch and booth both end on the receipt opening, then a silent bow from Tualang and no new claim. Small.


===== FILE: CLAUDE.md =====
# Lyceum: operating brief for AI sessions in this repository

Read these before doing anything, every session, in this order. Do not work from memory, from a summary or from an earlier conversation: the facts live in these files and they change.

1. [docs/lyceum-upgrade-plan.md](docs/lyceum-upgrade-plan.md), the master plan. Section 0 holds the rules, Sections 4, 4.1 and 4.2 the loop, the story and the roles, Section 6 the tokens, Section 7 the characters, Section 17 the packages and the session prompts.
2. [docs/competition/grand-final-2026.md](docs/competition/grand-final-2026.md), the date, the format, the criteria and what is still unconfirmed.
3. [docs/design/colour-scheme-2026-10-22.png](docs/design/colour-scheme-2026-10-22.png), the palette and type poster, with [docs/design/colour-scheme-audit-2026-10-22.md](docs/design/colour-scheme-audit-2026-10-22.md), the WCAG audit that says where each colour may appear; together they are the source of `src/theme/tokens.ts`.
4. [docs/research/](docs/research/), the dated research notes. Every figure on any surface comes from one of them or from `docs/claims.md` once it exists.
5. [DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md), why things are the way they are, and [docs/lyceum-improvement-plan.md](docs/lyceum-improvement-plan.md) Section 30 for the dated execution records and the work-package numbers W00 to W17.
6. The code a package touches, read in full before it is edited.

## Do not assume

If a fact is not in one of those files or in the code, say that it is not, then verify it or ask. Never fill a gap from memory. Weekdays are computed, not guessed. Counts come from running the command. Hex values come from `tokens.ts`. A claim about what works comes from a check that ran in this session. A memory file is a pointer to these references, never a source of facts.

## Rules that bind every session

- No en or em dashes anywhere: code strings, docs, captions, commit messages, slides.
- Honesty labels on every surface: real, prototype, illustrative, planned. Synthetic data says synthetic. A door is planned until a pilot tenant runs it.
- Colours and type come from `src/theme/tokens.ts` only; fonts are bundled, never fetched at runtime.
- No model-written text inside shipped artefacts before the pilot; model streaming is optional, labelled, and never moves a number.
- Verify before claiming: `npm run typecheck`, `npm run prove:all`, `npm run build`, the four smokes (`npm run smoke`, `smoke:integrity`, `smoke:workspace`, `smoke:curriculum`) and `node scripts/generate-guide.mjs --check`.
- Commit only when the owner asks. The team repository is reached only through `node scripts/team.mjs`; never `git push` between the two repositories by hand.
- Scan any third-party skill, plugin or MCP server with SkillSpector before first use (the rule is in the owner's global CLAUDE.md).
- Characters never appear on errors, billing or security moments, and nothing animates in the workspace without a real request or a saved record.
- Codex (`gpt-6-astra`) reviews plans and documents in a read-only sandbox with the files embedded through stdin; it never writes code into this repository, and its claims are verified before any is kept.

## Where things are

```text
docs/lyceum-upgrade-plan.md   the master plan (22 October 2026)
docs/competition/             the Grand Final facts
docs/design/                  the colour and type poster
docs/research/                dated research notes with sources
docs/review/                  Codex reviews, kept as written
docs/history/                 superseded documents, screenshots and presentations
src/lib/                      the deterministic core
src/app/                      store, roles, journey, runs
src/ui/                       primitives, shell, screens, and the characters once drawn
src/prove-*.ts                the checks; scripts/*-smoke.mjs the browser runs
server/                       the local API: parsing, board collection, optional model streaming
scripts/team.mjs              the only way to the team repository
```


===== FILE: docs/as-built.md (Sections 1, 2 and 6) =====
## 1. What Lyceum is

Lyceum turns real job postings into a course decision with receipts, every semester, inside the tools lecturers already use. A lecturer runs one loop per course and term: **Watch** (postings fetched on request from a permitted board, each with its link, date and fingerprint), **Report** (exact quotations beside their sources, the reviewed denominator, counts with Wilson intervals, a mapping to the outcomes, a brief), **Decide** (an immutable decision record by a person with the right to decide, bound to the brief, the version and the term), **Update** (a revision checked against the hours and the protected topics, adopted as a new version), **Teach** (sessions and an editable teaching pack with no model text) and **Learn** (results matched to the version taught, with n of N, and no learning gain claimed). The core is deterministic and needs no AI key; an assistant reaches it through the local, read-only door in `mcp/`. A host and seven helpers, drawn in code, make the steps visible; their moods follow records, never activity.

